{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0195EZQQiETj2PbwgxWNvjEi","type":"tool_result","content":"artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T190053/best/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T190053/median/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T190053/worst/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T222113/best/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T222113/median/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T222113/worst/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032813/best/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032813/median/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032813/worst/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044739/best/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044739/median/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044739/worst/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/analysis/cross_model_reml.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T075006/best/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T075006/median/experiment_data.json\nartifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T075006/worst/experiment_data.json"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tr8DtNDEYZrcPKiDisHLFC","session_id":"2badbab1-f12e-49d2-a949-e349aaeb2aba","uuid":"d179585b-e2a6-48a9-ac41-1c682ab8f0a6","timestamp":"2026-05-21T08:40:31.683Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015DTs5ncvm7CdFLcUg2fBzs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CLeRy69Xs1yjsxu6QVhpMi","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a peer review of a research paper submitted to AAAI. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Scoring Scale (AAAI 1-10)\n- 10: Technically flawless, groundbreaking impact\n- 9: Technically solid, high impact\n- 8: Strong paper, good evaluation\n- 7: Good paper, minor issues\n- 6: Marginally above threshold, some concerns\n- 5: Marginally below threshold\n- 4: Below threshold, significant issues\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivially wrong\n\n## Your Review Dimensions\nScore each on 1-10:\n1. **Novelty** — How new are the ideas? Is this an incremental extension or a genuine contribution?\n2. **Significance** — Will this matter to the community? Does it change practice or understanding?\n3. **Related work coverage** — Is prior work adequately discussed? Are key references missing?\n4. **Positioning** — Is the contribution clearly differentiated from prior work?\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Reproducibility Budgets for LLM Evaluation: A Generalizability Theory Analysis of Variance Sources Across Benchmarks and Models\"\n\n**Abstract:** Applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation, crossing infrastructure (precision) with methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering) and measurement (item) across 5 benchmarks and 6 models. Key findings: item selection = 42.1% variance on MMLU, model×item = 33.6%, variance structure is benchmark-conditional, G-theory needs 2.8–4.7× more items than IRT. Releases open-source pipeline.\n\n**4 Contributions:**\n1. Model×item interaction quantification (33.6%, CI [28.4, 37.9]) on MMLU\n2. Benchmark-conditional variance structure across 5 benchmarks  \n3. IRT vs G-theory comparison (2.8–4.7× ratio)\n4. Practitioner toolkit\n\n## Introduction Claims\n- Prior work isolates 1-2 variance sources, implicitly assuming separability across benchmarks and no interactions\n- Whether variance structure itself is stable across benchmarks remains untested\n- G-theory offers principled framework for simultaneous quantification of multiple variance sources + interactions\n- Mesting (2026) adapted G-theory to LLM evaluation but treated infrastructure as fixed and analyzed single benchmark\n- This work extends Mesting (2026) by: (a) crossing infrastructure with methodology, (b) analyzing multiple benchmarks, (c) treating model as crossed facet\n\n## Related Work (Section 2)\n\n**Variance sources in LLM evaluation:**\n- Sclar et al. (2024): up to 76% accuracy swings from prompt formatting. ICLR.\n- Brittlebench (2026): decomposed variance into data-intrinsic and brittleness, ~50% to prompt sensitivity\n- Yuan et al. (2025): up to 9% accuracy variation from numerical precision\n- ThinkingMachines (2025): batch-size-dependent nondeterminism from RMSNorm/GEMM kernels\n- Messina and Scotta (2026): background randomness at temperature zero across API providers. TMLR.\n- Limitation noted: \"each address one or two factors; none crosses infrastructure with methodology\"\n\n**Variance decomposition frameworks:**\n- Bouthillier et al. (2021): ANOVA for ML benchmarks across hyperparameters, data splits, seeds. JMLR.\n- Mesting (2026): G-theory to LLM evaluation with 5 methodological facets, single benchmark, infrastructure fixed\n- Kunievsky and Evans (2025): intent, articulation, model uncertainty decomposition\n- Paper claims to extend Mesting by (a) crossing infrastructure+methodology, (b) multiple benchmarks, (c) model as crossed facet\n\n**IRT and item-level analysis:**\n- Lalor et al. (2019), Rodriguez et al. (2021), Vania et al. (2021): IRT-based item analysis\n- Polo et al. (2024): IRT to recommend ~100 item subsets for efficient evaluation\n- Limitation: \"do not account for prompt, seed, or ordering variation\"\n\n**Reproducibility and fine-tuning instability:**\n- Dodge et al. (2020), Sellam et al. (2022): seed sensitivity in fine-tuning\n- Bui et al. (2025): RTES variance 18.22 for RoBERTa on COPA\n- Henderson et al. (2018): deep RL variance decomposition\n- Dehghani et al. (2021): benchmark lottery\n\n**References list (22 total):**\nBouthillier+2021(JMLR), Brennan2001, Brittlebench2026, Bui+2025, Dehghani+2021, Dodge+2020(EMNLP), Henderson+2018(AAAI), Hua+2025(EMNLP), Jiang&Skorupski2017, Kunievsky&Evans2025, Lalor+2019(NAACL), Lu+2022(ACL), Messina&Scotta2026(TMLR), Mesting2026, Mushquash&O'Connor2006, Perlitz+2023, Pezeshkpour&Hruschka2024, Polo+2024, Rodriguez+2021(ACL), Sclar+2024(ICLR), Sellam+2022, Shavelson&Webb1989,1991, ThinkingMachines2025, Vania+2021(ACL), Yuan+2025, Biderman+2024\n\n## Key Results for Assessing Novelty/Significance\n\n1. **Model×item = 33.6%**: First quantification in G-theory framework showing item difficulty is substantially model-specific (not just item-intrinsic)\n2. **Benchmark-conditional variance**: Item dominance ranges 28.1%–42.1%, model×item exceeds item on reasoning benchmarks — no universal reproducibility recipe\n3. **IRT/G-theory ratio 2.8–4.7×**: First direct comparison showing IRT underestimates item requirements because it ignores prompt/seed/ordering dimensions\n4. **D-study validation Δ=0.004**: Demonstrates out-of-sample predictive validity of G-theory projections for LLM evaluation\n5. **Separability evidence**: Precision×methodology <2% across benchmarks (binary scoring)\n6. **Fine-tuning variance**: Learning rate replaces item as dominant source; qualitatively different hierarchy\n\n## What Mesting (2026) Did vs. This Paper\n- Mesting: G-theory on single benchmark (safety eval), 5 facets (prompt, judge, temperature, seed, item), judge variance 43.8%, infrastructure FIXED\n- This paper: G-theory on 5 benchmarks, infrastructure CROSSED with methodology, model as random facet, model×item interaction quantified, D-study validated, IRT comparison, fine-tuning extension\n\n## Other Relevant Context\n- Biderman et al. (2024): \"Lessons from the trenches on reproducible evaluation\" — cited in references\n- Perlitz et al. (2023): \"Efficient benchmarking of language models\" — cited\n- The paper argues for a paradigm shift from \"how many items?\" (IRT question) to \"how many items × prompts × seeds?\" (G-theory question)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n1. Summary (2-3 sentences)\n2. Strengths (bulleted, focus on novelty/significance/related work)\n3. Weaknesses (bulleted, focus on novelty/significance/related work)\n4. Questions for authors\n5. Minor issues\n6. Scores for your 4 dimensions (1-10 each)\n7. Overall score (1-10) with justification\n8. Confidence (1-5, where 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Assess whether the novelty is sufficient for a top AI venue, whether the related work is complete, and whether the contributions are properly differentiated from prior art."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":444,"cache_read_input_tokens":59888,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":444},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2badbab1-f12e-49d2-a949-e349aaeb2aba","uuid":"1ab05b06-5cfb-4486-b253-f7f13789b5b7"}
