{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a5b238ba313530746","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CLeRy69Xs1yjsxu6QVhpMi","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":44175,"tool_uses":15,"duration_ms":116334},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"5ebcd0c9-647c-46ad-bc38-0a3428ce03f0","session_id":"2badbab1-f12e-49d2-a949-e349aaeb2aba"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y9Mf3pAWatmy4sQC7dNLno","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QTSV2QVrh3rSDcGmrbBsXh","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"nlp_variance_gtheory","dream_id":"dream_20260521T075006","tier":"best","overall_score":"7 — Good paper, minor issues","report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper applies Generalizability Theory (G-theory) from psychometrics to LLM evaluation, conducting a crossed factorial study of infrastructure (numerical precision), methodology (prompt, seed, temperature, ordering), and measurement (item) facets across 4 models and 5 benchmarks. The primary findings are that model×item interaction accounts for 33.6% of variance on MMLU (making item difficulty rankings substantially model-specific), that G-theory requires 2.8–4.7× more items than IRT to achieve reliable measurement, and that precision×methodology interactions are negligible under binary scoring. The paper releases an open-source G-theory pipeline and D-study budget calculator.\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Genuine methodological gap filled.** The crossing of infrastructure facets with methodology facets within a single G-theory framework is novel. Prior work (Mesting 2026, Brittlebench 2026, Yuan et al. 2025) each addresses one side; no existing study unifies them. Even though the result is a confirmatory null (separability holds under binary scoring), this is a useful empirical contribution — it validates the implicit assumption underlying every isolated-factor study in the literature.\n\n- **Model×item interaction is the standout finding.** The 33.6% model×item variance component, with the accompanying demonstration that item_id drops from 61% (single-model) to 42% (cross-model), is a well-quantified result with clear practical implications. The out-of-sample D-study validation (3-model prediction within Δ=0.004 of 4-model actual) is a particularly compelling methodological demonstration that goes beyond what Mesting (2026) offered.\n\n- **IRT vs. G-theory comparison is a genuine contribution.** The direct, quantified comparison showing G-theory requires 3.6–4.7× more items than IRT — and the clear explanation that this gap arises from prompt, seed, and ordering dimensions invisible to IRT — is novel and will be informative for the evaluation community. This bridges two literatures (IRT-based NLP evaluation and psychometric G-theory) that have been developing in parallel.\n\n- **Thorough related work.** The paper covers the relevant landscape comprehensively: variance-source studies (Sclar et al. 2024, Brittlebench 2026, Yuan et al. 2025, ThinkingMachines 2025, Messina & Scotta 2026), decomposition frameworks (Bouthillier et al. 2021, Mesting 2026, Kunievsky & Evans 2025), IRT approaches (Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021, Polo et al. 2024), and reproducibility literature (Dodge et al. 2020, Sellam et al. 2022, Henderson et al. 2018). The 22 references are adequate for a venue like AAAI.\n\n- **Honest positioning relative to Mesting (2026).** The paper correctly identifies itself as extending Mesting along three axes (infrastructure crossing, multiple benchmarks, model as crossed facet) rather than claiming to be an entirely new framework. This appropriate modesty strengthens credibility.\n\n- **Practical output.** The D-study budget calculator and open-source pipeline transform this from a purely diagnostic study into an actionable tool. The concrete budget tables (items × prompts × seeds for target G) are directly usable by practitioners designing evaluations.\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Cross-benchmark contribution is currently MMLU-only.** The abstract and title promise analysis \"across benchmarks and models,\" and the paper frames the benchmark-conditional variance structure as a primary contribution. However, the detailed quantitative results (345,600 records, 4 models, full crossed design) are only for MMLU. The other 4 benchmarks are mentioned as planned (exp-002) but not yet executed. If the submitted paper includes only MMLU with full results and reduced-design results for other benchmarks, the \"benchmark-conditional\" claim is significantly weakened. This is the single largest gap between the paper's framing and its current evidence base.\n\n- **\"Infrastructure\" is limited to numerical precision on one GPU architecture.** The paper acknowledges this, but the framing still occasionally implies broader infrastructure coverage. Hardware type (GPU model, cloud provider), batch size, and inference engine version are all infrastructure factors documented by Yuan et al. (2025) and ThinkingMachines (2025) that remain unexamined. The separability claim is really \"precision separability,\" not \"infrastructure separability.\" This narrows the novelty of Claim 2.\n\n- **Incremental over Mesting (2026) — the degree of extension needs careful calibration.** Mesting applied G-theory to LLM evaluation with 5 facets on a single benchmark. This paper adds (a) precision as a crossed facet, (b) multiple benchmarks (pending), and (c) model as a facet. Extension (a) yields a null result. Extension (b) is incomplete. Extension (c) is the strongest contribution (model×item = 33.6%), but treating model as a crossed random facet is a standard G-theory analysis choice, not a methodological innovation. The novelty is in the empirical finding, not the technique. This is fine for AAAI, but the paper must be careful not to overclaim methodological novelty.\n\n- **Missing related work: benchmark efficiency and adaptive testing.** The IRT comparison should also discuss:\n  - Zhuang et al. (2023), \"Efficiently Measuring the Cognitive Ability of LLMs\" — computerized adaptive testing for LLMs, a direct alternative to both fixed-form IRT and G-theory approaches.\n  - The broader CAT (computerized adaptive testing) literature, which also addresses the \"how many items?\" question from a different angle.\n  - Vivek et al. (2024) or similar work on dynamic benchmarking — if the variance structure is benchmark-conditional, adaptive approaches may outperform fixed budgets.\n\n- **Missing related work: multi-trait multi-method (MTMM).** G-theory with model as a crossed facet is conceptually close to a multi-trait multi-method matrix (Campbell & Fiske, 1959). The model×item interaction is analogous to trait×method variance. This connection to classical psychometric frameworks would strengthen the paper's theoretical grounding and is conspicuously absent.\n\n- **Sclar et al. (2024) deserves deeper engagement.** Currently cited for \"up to 76% accuracy swings\" but the paper's relationship to Sclar is more nuanced: Sclar documents the prompt sensitivity phenomenon, while this paper quantifies its relative contribution within a multi-facet framework. The paper should explicitly discuss what G-theory adds beyond what Sclar already showed — namely, the decomposition of prompt effects into main effects vs. prompt×item interactions, which Sclar's one-factor-at-a-time design cannot distinguish.\n\n- **No engagement with the \"benchmark lottery\" implications.** Dehghani et al. (2021) is cited but its central thesis — that benchmark selection itself is a source of variance in ML conclusions — is directly relevant to the benchmark-conditional variance finding. If variance structure differs across benchmarks, then benchmark selection is not just about coverage but about which variance regime the researcher operates in. This connection deserves more than a citation.\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Cross-benchmark evidence**: What is the current status of exp-002 (GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag)? If only MMLU results are available at submission, how will you support the \"benchmark-conditional\" claim? Will you present it as a hypothesis or a finding?\n\n2. **Model selection**: The 4 models (Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9B, Mistral-7B, Qwen3-8B) are all 7-9B open-weight instruction-tuned models. How confident are you that the model×item interaction magnitude generalizes to (a) models of different scales (e.g., 70B), (b) base vs. instruction-tuned, or (c) proprietary API models? The D-study projection to 10 models assumes the new models come from the same \"universe\" — is this justified?\n\n3. **Judge exclusion**: Mesting found judge model accounts for 43.8% of variance. You fix the judge to isolate other factors. But this means your variance decomposition and the resulting budgets apply only to fixed-judge evaluation setups. How would you expect the budget recommendations to change if judge were included as a facet?\n\n4. **Binary data suitability**: G-theory assumes continuous data. Your primary metric is binary (correct/incorrect). The Bernoulli non-artifact analysis (737.5× ratio) addresses one concern, but does not address whether variance component estimates are biased under binary data. Have you considered the logistic-link generalized G-theory approaches (Jiang & Skorupski, 2017) for a more principled treatment?\n\n5. **Practical adoption barrier**: The D-study recommends ~510 items for G≥0.80, but standard MMLU evaluation uses ~14K items. Is the 510-item recommendation for a subsample design? If so, how does this interact with the item sampling strategy (stratified vs. random)? The exp001b resampling study suggests robustness, but only for 200-item samples.\n\n## 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The title includes \"Evaluation Metrics\" but the dual-metric analysis (binary vs. text-exact-match) is more of a sensitivity analysis than a systematic study of metrics. Consider whether this phrase overpromises.\n- The confidence interval on model×item [28.4, 37.9] is relatively wide (9.5pp). Acknowledging this uncertainty more prominently would strengthen the paper's credibility.\n- The FP32 anomaly attributed to vLLM enforce_eager is an important methodological finding in its own right. Consider whether it warrants a brief discussion box or methodological note, as other researchers using vLLM may encounter the same issue.\n- Several references (Brittlebench 2026, ThinkingMachines 2025) lack author names in the reference list — ensure full bibliographic information.\n\n## 6. Dimension Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Novelty** | 7/10 | The infrastructure×methodology crossing within G-theory is genuinely new, though the null result limits its impact. Model×item quantification and the IRT comparison are solid novel contributions. Not a paradigm shift, but a meaningful advance. |\n| **Significance** | 7/10 | The D-study budgets and model×item findings will directly influence how the community designs evaluations. The IRT comparison bridges two important literatures. Impact is somewhat limited by MMLU-only full results and restriction to open-weight 7-9B models. |\n| **Related work coverage** | 7/10 | Comprehensive coverage of the immediate landscape (variance sources, G-theory, IRT). Missing CAT/adaptive testing literature, MTMM connection, and deeper engagement with Sclar and Dehghani. 22 references is adequate but could be stronger with 3-5 additional targeted citations. |\n| **Positioning** | 8/10 | The paper does an honest and effective job differentiating from Mesting (2026) along three axes. The framing of infrastructure crossing as confirmatory rather than breakthrough is appropriately calibrated. The IRT comparison is well-positioned. Slight deduction for occasional drift toward broader \"infrastructure\" claims when only precision is tested. |\n\n## 7. Overall Score: 7/10\n\n**Justification**: This is a good paper that fills a genuine gap in the LLM evaluation methodology literature. The application of G-theory with model as a crossed facet, the quantification of model×item interaction at 33.6%, the out-of-sample D-study validation, and the IRT comparison are collectively sufficient for a contribution at AAAI. The paper is honest in its positioning relative to Mesting (2026) and provides actionable outputs (budgets, toolkit).\n\nThe main risks to acceptance are: (1) the cross-benchmark claim is currently unsupported by full experimental results, which undermines the \"benchmark-conditional\" framing that the paper elevates as a primary contribution; (2) the infrastructure facet is narrow (precision only, single GPU), limiting the generality of the separability finding; and (3) some reviewers may view this as an incremental extension of Mesting (2026) — the paper needs to make the case that the model×item finding and IRT comparison are each individually sufficient contributions beyond what Mesting showed.\n\nIf the cross-benchmark results (exp-002) are included at submission with consistent findings, this moves to a strong 7 or weak 8. Without them, the paper rests primarily on the MMLU analysis, which is thorough but limits the scope of the claims.\n\n## 8. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have strong familiarity with the G-theory literature, IRT applications in NLP evaluation, and the LLM reproducibility/variance decomposition space. I am less certain about the very latest adaptive testing approaches for LLMs, which may contain additional relevant work I have not identified.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":738,"cache_read_input_tokens":43128,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":738,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CLeRy69Xs1yjsxu6QVhpMi","session_id":"2badbab1-f12e-49d2-a949-e349aaeb2aba","uuid":"e5658026-6704-443f-b1a4-ea98f64ee592"}
